# Two failures in one operation merge; one failure propagates as itself. The
# operation is a construction: an operator merged too until 2026-09-16, when
# a bare err at an operator became a halt, and the record is what is left of
# the 2026-08-05 rule that neither side of an operation caused the other.
#
# The reasons are read through `std/testing`'s `when_failed`, foreign to this
# module and therefore licensed to separate a reason from a failure this
# module raised.
#
# Since the 2026-09-15 ruling a raised err handed straight to an operator or
# to a group with no arm for it does not compile, so the failures here are
# bound to names first. The checker reads a call's arguments and not the names
# it is handed, the same deliberate blind spot the none rule keeps, and the
# runtime contract these lines pin is reached through it. Since 2026-09-16 an
# operator halts on a bare err rather than merging, so the merges here are
# constructions, which still merge: a record's fields are unordered and
# neither caused the other.
import "std/testing"

type couple
  left
  right

fn boom what
  err what

fn as_text reason
  "{reason}"

a = boom "a"

b = boom "b"

both = couple a b

left = couple a 1

right = couple 1 b

pub play =
  print "both: {testing/when_failed both as_text}"
  >> print "left: {testing/when_failed left as_text}"
  >> print "right: {testing/when_failed right as_text}"
